WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:06.410 --> 00:10.670
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

00:10.670 --> 00:16.890
Good afternoon.

00:16.890 --> 00:21.780
The Federal Open Market Committee concluded a two-day meeting earlier today.

00:21.780 --> 00:24.530
As always, my colleagues and I reviewed recent economic

00:24.530 --> 00:28.300
and financial developments and discussed the economic outlook.

00:28.300 --> 00:30.780
The data since our January meeting have been generally consistent

00:30.780 --> 00:33.670
with our expectation that the fourth quarter pause

00:33.670 --> 00:35.710
in the recovery would prove temporary

00:35.710 --> 00:39.410
and that moderate economic growth would resume.

00:39.410 --> 00:42.340
Spending by households and businesses has continued to expand,

00:42.340 --> 00:46.030
and the housing sector has seen further gains.

00:46.030 --> 00:49.370
The jobs market has also shown signs of improvement over the past six months

00:49.370 --> 00:52.680
or so: Private payrolls are growing more quickly,

00:52.680 --> 00:56.910
total hours of work have increased, the rate of filings of new claims

00:56.910 --> 00:59.730
for unemployment insurance has fallen,

00:59.730 --> 01:03.170
and the unemployment rate has continued to tick down.

01:03.170 --> 01:07.570
However, at 7.7 percent, the unemployment rate remains elevated.

01:07.570 --> 01:10.160
The Committee also remains concerned

01:10.160 --> 01:12.920
that restrictive fiscal policies may slow economic growth

01:12.920 --> 01:15.610
and job creation in coming months.

01:15.610 --> 01:19.900
We continue to monitor the recent increases in gasoline prices,

01:19.900 --> 01:22.530
which appear to be due mostly to passing factors

01:22.530 --> 01:25.850
such as refinery shutdowns for maintenance.

01:25.850 --> 01:28.180
Apart from temporary variations in energy prices,

01:28.180 --> 01:31.440
inflation is running somewhat below the Committee's longer-run objective

01:31.440 --> 01:33.600
of 2 percent.

01:33.600 --> 01:38.030
Importantly, longer-term inflation expectations remain stable.

01:38.030 --> 01:43.000
Overall, still-high unemployment, in combination with relatively low inflation,

01:43.000 --> 01:44.790
underscores the need for policies

01:44.790 --> 01:48.100
that will support progress towards maximum employment

01:48.100 --> 01:51.570
in a context of price stability.

01:51.570 --> 01:52.770
In conjunction with this meeting,

01:52.770 --> 01:56.120
the 19 participants in our policy discussion-the 7 Board members

01:56.120 --> 02:01.940
and 12 Reserve Bank presidents-submitted individual economic projections.

02:01.940 --> 02:06.360
As always, each participant's projections are conditioned on his or her own view

02:06.360 --> 02:09.410
of appropriate monetary policy.

02:09.410 --> 02:11.220
To summarize, the participants' projections

02:11.220 --> 02:18.190
for economic growth have a central tendency of 2.3 to 2.8 percent for 2013,

02:18.190 --> 02:23.450
rising to 2.9 to 3.7 percent in 2015.

02:23.450 --> 02:26.190
The central tendency of their projections of the unemployment rate

02:26.190 --> 02:31.600
for the fourth quarter of this year is 7.3 to 7.5 percent,

02:31.600 --> 02:36.740
declining to 6.0 to 6.5 percent in the final quarter of 2015.

02:36.740 --> 02:41.630
Most participants see inflation gradually increasing toward the Committee's

02:41.630 --> 02:44.980
longer-run target; the central tendency of their projections

02:44.980 --> 02:54.650
for inflation is 1.3 to 1.7 percent this year and 1.7 to 2.0 percent in 2015.

02:54.650 --> 02:56.390
As you already know from the policy statement,

02:56.390 --> 03:00.900
we are continuing the asset purchase program first announced in September.

03:00.900 --> 03:05.270
This decision was supported by our review at the meeting of the likely efficacy,

03:05.270 --> 03:09.170
costs, and risks of additional purchases.

03:09.170 --> 03:14.450
Let me briefly summarize the cost-benefit analysis supporting our decision.

03:14.450 --> 03:16.190
Although estimates of the efficacy

03:16.190 --> 03:19.670
of the Federal Reserve's asset purchases are necessarily uncertain,

03:19.670 --> 03:23.410
most participants agreed that these purchases-by putting downward pressure

03:23.410 --> 03:26.620
on longer-term interest rates, including mortgage rates-continue

03:26.620 --> 03:31.330
to provide meaningful support to economic growth and job creation.

03:31.330 --> 03:35.420
However, most also agreed that this monetary tool would likely not be able

03:35.420 --> 03:39.480
on its own to fully offset major economic headwinds,

03:39.480 --> 03:43.430
such as those that might arise from significant near-term fiscal restraint

03:43.430 --> 03:47.710
or from a sharp increase in global financial stresses.

03:47.710 --> 03:50.290
We also had a thorough discussion of possible costs

03:50.290 --> 03:54.580
and risks of continued expansion of the Federal Reserve's balance sheet.

03:54.580 --> 03:58.540
The risks include possible adverse implications of additional purchases

03:58.540 --> 04:00.970
for the functioning of securities markets,

04:00.970 --> 04:05.270
and the potential effects-under various scenarios-of a larger balance sheet

04:05.270 --> 04:08.360
on the Federal Reserve's earnings from its asset holdings and, hence,

04:08.360 --> 04:11.170
on its remittances to the Treasury.

04:11.170 --> 04:15.230
The Committee also considered possible risks to financial stability,

04:15.230 --> 04:19.290
such as might arise if persistently low rates lead some market participants

04:19.290 --> 04:22.690
to take on excessive risk in a "reach for yield."

04:22.690 --> 04:28.590
In the Committee's view, these costs remain manageable but will continue

04:28.590 --> 04:32.850
to be monitored, and we will take them into appropriate account

04:32.850 --> 04:36.720
as we determine the size, pace, and composition of our asset purchases.

04:36.720 --> 04:41.860
As for today, our policy decision had two main elements.

04:41.860 --> 04:43.170
First, the Committee decided

04:43.170 --> 04:47.050
to continue purchasing additional agency mortgage-backed securities at a pace

04:47.050 --> 04:51.050
of $40 billion per month and longer-term Treasury securities

04:51.050 --> 04:54.180
at a pace of $45 billion per month.

04:54.180 --> 04:58.540
It bears emphasizing that the Committee has described this program in terms

04:58.540 --> 05:00.730
of a monthly pace of purchases,

05:00.730 --> 05:03.860
rather than as a total amount of expected purchases,

05:03.860 --> 05:08.530
and has tied the evolution of the program to economic criteria-specifically,

05:08.530 --> 05:11.040
to the achievement of a substantial improvement in the outlook

05:11.040 --> 05:14.360
for the labor market in a context of price stability.

05:14.360 --> 05:19.240
Within this framework, the Committee could vary the pace of purchases

05:19.240 --> 05:23.450
as progress is made toward its economic objectives or if its assessment

05:23.450 --> 05:27.340
of the efficacy and costs of the program changes.

05:27.340 --> 05:30.880
At this meeting, the Committee judged that no adjustment was warranted.

05:30.880 --> 05:36.200
Second, the Committee kept the target for the federal funds rate at 0

05:36.200 --> 05:40.460
to ¼ percent and reaffirmed its expectation that a highly accommodative stance

05:40.460 --> 05:44.170
of monetary policy will remain appropriate for a considerable time

05:44.170 --> 05:48.950
after the asset purchase program ends and the economic recovery strengthens.

05:48.950 --> 05:52.700
In particular, we anticipate that this exceptionally low range

05:52.700 --> 05:55.570
for the funds rate will be appropriate at least as long

05:55.570 --> 05:59.800
as the unemployment rate remains above 6½ percent, inflation between one

05:59.800 --> 06:03.630
and two years ahead is projected to be no more than a half percentage point

06:03.630 --> 06:07.180
above the Committee's longer-run inflation goal of 2 percent,

06:07.180 --> 06:10.590
and longer-term inflation expectations continue to be well anchored.

06:10.590 --> 06:13.850
I should note, as I have on other occasions,

06:13.850 --> 06:15.350
that the economic conditions provided

06:15.350 --> 06:19.700
in this forward guidance are thresholds, not triggers.

06:19.700 --> 06:22.650
Crossing one or more of these thresholds will not lead automatically

06:22.650 --> 06:25.010
to an increase in rates.

06:25.010 --> 06:26.360
Rather, the Committee will assess

06:26.360 --> 06:30.010
at that time whether the outlook justifies raising its target

06:30.010 --> 06:32.370
for the federal funds rate.

06:32.370 --> 06:35.830
This guidance will help market participants assess how the Federal Reserve's

06:35.830 --> 06:39.630
interest rate policy is likely to respond to economic developments,

06:39.630 --> 06:42.920
but its broader purpose is to assure households and businesses

06:42.920 --> 06:47.150
that monetary policy will continue to support the recovery even as the pace

06:47.150 --> 06:50.740
of economic growth and job creation picks up.

06:50.740 --> 06:52.180
In their individual projections,

06:52.180 --> 06:56.850
14 of the 19 FOMC participants saw the first increase in the target

06:56.850 --> 07:01.480
for the federal funds rate as occurring in 2015 or 2016.

07:01.480 --> 07:05.690
Let me comment briefly on how the two main pieces

07:05.690 --> 07:09.690
of our policy accommodation-asset purchases and guidance about future changes

07:09.690 --> 07:12.250
in the federal funds rate-fit together.

07:12.250 --> 07:15.100
The purpose of the asset purchases is

07:15.100 --> 07:17.550
to increase the economy's near-term momentum,

07:17.550 --> 07:20.440
with the goal of improving the outlook for the labor market

07:20.440 --> 07:25.430
and helping to promote a self-sustaining recovery with price stability.

07:25.430 --> 07:27.360
The forward rate guidance, in turn,

07:27.360 --> 07:30.570
provides information about when the Committee will begin considering the removal

07:30.570 --> 07:33.750
of policy accommodation through increases in the target

07:33.750 --> 07:36.090
for the federal funds rate.

07:36.090 --> 07:39.920
Importantly, the Committee expects a considerable interval to pass

07:39.920 --> 07:42.400
between the time when the Committee will cease adding accommodation

07:42.400 --> 07:45.470
through asset purchases and the time when it will be appropriate

07:45.470 --> 07:47.260
to begin removing accommodation

07:47.260 --> 07:51.530
by moving the federal funds rate target toward more normal levels.

07:51.530 --> 07:54.430
As always, in deciding on the appropriate stance of policy,

07:54.430 --> 07:58.280
the Committee will take a balanced approach consistent with its longer-run goals

07:58.280 --> 08:01.650
of maximum employment and inflation of 2 percent.

08:01.650 --> 08:06.090
In sum, the Committee anticipates moderate economic growth,

08:06.090 --> 08:10.520
supported by household and business spending and a strengthening housing sector.

08:10.520 --> 08:13.800
The labor market has shown signs of improvement in recent months,

08:13.800 --> 08:17.530
but the unemployment rate remains elevated.

08:17.530 --> 08:19.180
Inflation is expected to remain low,

08:19.180 --> 08:23.340
and fiscal policy has become somewhat more restrictive.

08:23.340 --> 08:26.190
In light of its outlook, and following a review of the efficacy

08:26.190 --> 08:28.760
and costs of additional asset purchases,

08:28.760 --> 08:31.760
the Committee today reaffirmed its asset purchase program

08:31.760 --> 08:33.810
and its federal funds guidance.

08:33.810 --> 08:34.560
Thank you very much.

08:34.560 --> 08:41.480
I would be glad to take your questions.

08:42.570 --> 08:45.920
YLAN MUI. Hi.

08:45.920 --> 08:48.140
Ylan Mui, Washington Post.

08:48.140 --> 08:50.070
My question is around QE.

08:50.070 --> 08:53.760
Obviously, we've seen some of your colleagues give more-specific criteria,

08:53.760 --> 08:55.300
give some color around what they're looking

08:55.300 --> 08:59.230
for before they would consider exiting from QE.

08:59.230 --> 09:02.670
Can you give us any additional color on what you're looking for specifically

09:02.670 --> 09:05.490
in terms of substantial improvement in the labor market?

09:05.490 --> 09:10.330
And does the fact that there aren't thresholds associated with QE say anything

09:10.330 --> 09:14.340
about the level of disagreement among the Committee members over what

09:14.340 --> 09:15.080
that exit should look like?

09:15.080 --> 09:16.260
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

09:16.260 --> 09:19.550
Well, to take your second question first,

09:19.550 --> 09:25.020
the lack of thresholds comes from the complexity of the problem.

09:25.020 --> 09:28.190
On the one hand, we have benefits, which are associated with improvements

09:28.190 --> 09:32.900
in the economy, but there are also costs associated with unconventional policy,

09:32.900 --> 09:38.690
such as potential effects on financial stability, which are hard to quantify

09:38.690 --> 09:40.560
and which people have different views about.

09:40.560 --> 09:45.090
So, to this point, we've not been able to give quantitative thresholds

09:45.090 --> 09:47.650
for the asset purchases in the same way that we have

09:47.650 --> 09:49.490
for the federal funds rate target.

09:49.490 --> 09:52.930
We're going to continue to try to provide information as we go forward.

09:52.930 --> 09:59.350
In particular, as I mentioned today, as we make progress towards our objective,

09:59.350 --> 10:03.300
we may adjust the flow rate of purchases month to month

10:03.300 --> 10:05.680
to appropriately calibrate the amount

10:05.680 --> 10:12.800
of accommodation we're providing given the outlook for the labor market.

10:12.800 --> 10:17.980
In terms of further color, again, given the complexity of the issue,

10:17.980 --> 10:22.010
we've not given quantitative analysis or quantitative thresholds.

10:22.010 --> 10:27.440
I would say that we'll be looking for sustained improvement in a range

10:27.440 --> 10:31.770
of key labor market indicators, including, obviously, payrolls,

10:31.770 --> 10:35.320
unemployment rate, but also others like the hiring rate,

10:35.320 --> 10:39.710
claims for unemployment insurance, quit rates, wage rates, and so on.

10:39.710 --> 10:43.380
We'll be looking for sustained improvement across a range of indicators,

10:43.380 --> 10:48.070
and in a way that's taking place throughout the economy.

10:48.070 --> 10:51.290
And since we're looking at the outlook-we're looking at the prospects rather

10:51.290 --> 10:54.810
than the current state of the labor market- we'll also be looking at things

10:54.810 --> 10:57.930
like growth to try to understand whether there's sufficient momentum

10:57.930 --> 11:00.860
in the economy to provide demand for labor going forward.

11:00.860 --> 11:03.030
So that will allow us to look through, perhaps,

11:03.030 --> 11:10.030
some temporary fluctuations associated with short-term shocks or problems.

11:10.030 --> 11:12.450
ROBIN HARDING.

11:14.410 --> 11:18.340
Robin Harding from the Financial Times.

11:18.340 --> 11:21.560
Mr. Chairman, to follow up on that last point,

11:21.560 --> 11:24.920
what-you referred to the possibility of varying purchases,

11:24.920 --> 11:26.850
the rates of purchases per month.

11:26.850 --> 11:30.960
What's the difference in the conditions that would induce you to do that,

11:30.960 --> 11:33.950
as opposed to the substantial improvements in the labor market

11:33.950 --> 11:36.280
that would induce you to stop the program altogether?

11:36.280 --> 11:38.000
Thank you.

11:38.000 --> 11:38.710
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

11:38.710 --> 11:41.880
Well, the problem with having just a single criterion is

11:41.880 --> 11:44.210
that it's all or nothing.

11:44.210 --> 11:46.830
We maintain full speed ahead until we hit a certain target,

11:46.830 --> 11:48.920
and then, you know, we stop.

11:48.920 --> 11:51.010
That would be, I think, very difficult for the markets

11:51.010 --> 11:52.830
to understand, to anticipate.

11:52.830 --> 11:56.460
We think it makes more sense to have our policy variable,

11:56.460 --> 12:01.050
which is the rate of flow of purchases, respond in a more continuous

12:01.050 --> 12:03.870
or sensitive way to changes in the outlook.

12:03.870 --> 12:08.090
So, as we make progress towards our ultimate objective

12:08.090 --> 12:12.110
of substantial improvement, we may adjust the rate

12:12.110 --> 12:14.290
of flow of purchases accordingly.

12:14.290 --> 12:17.830
Now, we won't do that every meeting; we won't do that frequently.

12:17.830 --> 12:23.300
But when we see that the condition or the situation has changed

12:23.300 --> 12:28.130
in a meaningful way, then we may well adjust the pace of purchases in order

12:28.130 --> 12:32.060
to keep the level of accommodation consistent with the outlook, and, secondly,

12:32.060 --> 12:34.590
to help provide the markets with some sense

12:34.590 --> 12:36.580
of progress-how much progress is being made

12:36.580 --> 12:37.930
so that it can make better judgments.

12:37.930 --> 12:38.360
ROBIN HARDING.

12:38.360 --> 12:43.740
Could you define "progress towards" at all, how you deal with that?

12:43.740 --> 12:44.610
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

12:44.610 --> 12:48.430
Well, you know, as I just described what I mean by "substantial improvement,"

12:48.430 --> 12:54.490
it's a broad-based improvement in a range of indicators as well as improvement

12:54.490 --> 12:56.850
in output and labor demand.

12:56.850 --> 13:00.010
So, as we move-we see partial improvements, see modest improvement,

13:00.010 --> 13:04.060
as we see a period in which the labor market is doing better,

13:04.060 --> 13:06.270
and we have reason to think it might be stronger,

13:06.270 --> 13:10.240
then we might reduce accommodation at that point.

13:10.240 --> 13:11.580
But it works in both directions.

13:11.580 --> 13:14.010
If, subsequently, the labor market were to weaken,

13:14.010 --> 13:16.140
the outlook were to get worse, we could, of course,

13:16.140 --> 13:22.340
bring back accommodation back to the previous level.

13:24.330 --> 13:25.180
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:25.180 --> 13:26.470
Steve Liesman from CNBC.

13:26.470 --> 13:29.320
Mr. Chairman, I have to keep coming back to this issue

13:29.320 --> 13:30.600
of adjusting the flow rate.

13:30.600 --> 13:33.470
Are we near that time right now?

13:33.470 --> 13:38.590
And how can the markets calibrate the number to changes in economic improvement?

13:38.590 --> 13:42.790
For example, let's say, theoretically, we did 236,000 jobs in a month,

13:42.790 --> 13:45.480
and the unemployment rate fell by 0.2 percent.

13:45.480 --> 13:51.000
Would that be sufficient to begin to adjust the purchases downward?

13:51.000 --> 13:51.510
Thank you.

13:51.510 --> 13:52.010
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

13:52.010 --> 13:54.440
Well, that's going to be obviously a decision that the Committee has to make,

13:54.440 --> 13:57.880
and we will-at each meeting, we'll look at progress that's been made

13:57.880 --> 14:00.430
since the last meeting, try to assess the outlook,

14:00.430 --> 14:02.980
try to determine whether there's been a sufficient change

14:02.980 --> 14:07.200
to warrant a change in our policy stance.

14:07.200 --> 14:12.330
Internally, we'll use models and other indicators of the state

14:12.330 --> 14:18.150
of the labor market to try to make a good estimate of how much we need

14:18.150 --> 14:20.830
to change the rate of flow.

14:20.830 --> 14:25.120
But, again, the point of this is to let the market see our behavior,

14:25.120 --> 14:28.080
to let them see how we respond to changes in the outlook.

14:28.080 --> 14:31.170
And that way there will be a better ability, I hope,

14:31.170 --> 14:37.330
for the markets to anticipate either a return to higher levels of purchases

14:37.330 --> 14:39.290
or the ultimate phasing-out of the program.

14:39.290 --> 14:39.890
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:39.890 --> 14:41.570
Are we near that level now?

14:41.570 --> 14:41.950
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

14:41.950 --> 14:45.010
That's an issue for-obviously, there has been improvement, let me say that.

14:45.010 --> 14:48.290
We've seen improvement in the last four or five months.

14:48.290 --> 14:49.850
The last five months, for example,

14:49.850 --> 14:53.090
we've seen over 200,000 jobs a month in the private sector.

14:53.090 --> 14:57.300
Unemployment rate has come down four-tenths since September.

14:57.300 --> 15:02.440
Unemployment claims, insurance claims are at the lowest level they've been

15:02.440 --> 15:05.260
since the crisis, so we are seeing improvement.

15:05.260 --> 15:07.800
I think one thing we would need is to make sure

15:07.800 --> 15:11.220
that this is not a temporary improvement.

15:11.220 --> 15:17.140
So, we've seen periods before where we had as many as 300,000 jobs for a couple

15:17.140 --> 15:19.920
of months, and then things weakened again.

15:19.920 --> 15:24.000
So, I think an important criterion would be not just the improvement

15:24.000 --> 15:29.110
that we've seen, but is it going to be sustained for a number of months?

15:29.110 --> 15:32.640
PEDRO DA COSTA.

15:32.640 --> 15:34.070
Pedro da Costa from Reuters.

15:34.070 --> 15:37.640
Mr. Chairman, I wanted to ask you about Cyprus a little bit,

15:37.640 --> 15:40.360
because you guys removed the reference in the statement referring

15:40.360 --> 15:43.100
to easing financial conditions,

15:43.100 --> 15:47.170
suggesting that you are alert to these new risks that seem to have emerged.

15:47.170 --> 15:50.600
Doesn't the fact that a country as small as Cyprus can set off

15:50.600 --> 15:53.560
such global reverberations suggest

15:53.560 --> 15:56.300
that the financial system is perhaps a lot more fragile

15:56.300 --> 15:59.860
than the Fed stress test suggests?

15:59.860 --> 16:05.190
And then, separately, you discounted the estimate of the "too big

16:05.190 --> 16:07.480
to fail" subsidy that Elizabeth Warren threw

16:07.480 --> 16:09.830
at you during the Senate hearing a couple weeks ago.

16:09.830 --> 16:13.360
I was wondering, does the Fed have its own estimate of what that subsidy is,

16:13.360 --> 16:14.840
and could you tell us what it is, if that's the case?

16:14.840 --> 16:15.200
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

16:15.200 --> 16:20.560
Well, first, on the reverberations, it's a difficult situation in Cyprus.

16:20.560 --> 16:24.310
You've got a situation where the banking system is a large multiple

16:24.310 --> 16:26.050
of the size of the economy.

16:26.050 --> 16:30.850
And so, in the financial sense, it's bigger than it is in a GDP sense.

16:30.850 --> 16:35.120
And it's a difficult problem because the country faces both fiscal

16:35.120 --> 16:38.370
and bank capitalization issues.

16:38.370 --> 16:41.930
And you've seen that there's political stress in terms of trying to figure

16:41.930 --> 16:45.910
out how they're going to meet the demands of the euro group

16:45.910 --> 16:49.570
for contributing to their rescue.

16:49.570 --> 16:51.680
So, there are a lot of uncertainties and difficulties,

16:51.680 --> 16:57.450
and there's questions about how-the way Cyprus is treated,

16:57.450 --> 16:59.650
what implications that might have for other countries and the like.

16:59.650 --> 17:02.280
So, it does have some consequence.

17:02.280 --> 17:07.530
But, having said that, you know, the vote failed and the markets are up today,

17:07.530 --> 17:11.200
and I don't think that the impact has been enormous.

17:11.200 --> 17:13.700
I mean, I think it's something we're paying attention to.

17:13.700 --> 17:16.880
We hope that the Europeans will come

17:16.880 --> 17:21.700
up with an efficient and equitable solution.

17:21.700 --> 17:24.320
We are monitoring very carefully, but, at this point,

17:24.320 --> 17:33.660
we're not seeing a major risk to the U.S. financial system or the U.S. economy.

17:33.660 --> 17:38.470
On the benefits of being too big to fail, no, we don't have an estimate.

17:38.470 --> 17:41.190
It's pretty difficult to control for all the factors that go

17:41.190 --> 17:47.670
into determining the size of the subsidy.

17:47.670 --> 17:50.890
I think there is some evidence that financial markets are,

17:50.890 --> 17:54.850
at least to some extent, taking into account the possibility

17:54.850 --> 17:56.750
that large financial institutions will fail.

17:56.750 --> 17:59.590
You see, for example, spreads in the credit default swaps

17:59.590 --> 18:02.630
that indicate some probability of failure.

18:02.630 --> 18:06.100
You see some discrimination among different institutions according

18:06.100 --> 18:08.830
to the bond market-interest rates that they get charged, and so on.

18:08.830 --> 18:12.600
So there is some evidence of market discrimination.

18:12.600 --> 18:16.080
That being said, I certainly never meant to say

18:16.080 --> 18:19.050
to Senator Warren-and I share her concern about too-big-to-fail,

18:19.050 --> 18:22.000
I think it's a major issue-I never meant to imply

18:22.000 --> 18:23.350
that the problem was solved and gone.

18:23.350 --> 18:28.680
It is not solved and gone; it's still here, but there's a lot of work in train.

18:28.680 --> 18:31.850
We're putting in the Basel capital standards.

18:31.850 --> 18:35.970
We're putting in the orderly liquidation authority from Dodd-Frank.

18:35.970 --> 18:37.790
We're working with our international partners.

18:37.790 --> 18:41.080
And I hope that we'll make progress against too-big-to-fail,

18:41.080 --> 18:46.630
because I agree with her 100 percent that it's a real problem

18:46.630 --> 18:49.180
and needs to be addressed if at all possible.

18:49.180 --> 18:55.330
JON HILSENRATH.

18:55.330 --> 18:56.950
Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

18:56.950 --> 19:00.090
Mr. Chairman, I'd like to change the subject a little.

19:00.090 --> 19:04.940
Your predecessor served as Fed Chairman for 19 years.

19:04.940 --> 19:09.030
By contrast, the European Central Bank

19:09.030 --> 19:14.810
and the Bank of England have 8-year term limits on-for their top people.

19:14.810 --> 19:17.090
I wanted to ask you two questions related to that.

19:17.090 --> 19:21.040
First, as a matter of governance, do you think 8 years is the right amount

19:21.040 --> 19:25.560
of time for a central bank leader to serve,

19:25.560 --> 19:28.950
or should it be less or more, or undefined?

19:28.950 --> 19:31.480
And, more specifically, as it regards to you,

19:31.480 --> 19:34.210
your term is 10 years from completion.

19:34.210 --> 19:37.810
How are you thinking about what you will be doing next year

19:37.810 --> 19:39.750
after your term is completed?

19:39.750 --> 19:41.010
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

19:41.010 --> 19:44.450
Well, on the latter question, I don't have anything for you.

19:44.450 --> 19:50.050
I'll certainly be informing the Wall Street Journal and other publications

19:50.050 --> 19:54.780
if I come to some decision or some developments in that front.

19:54.780 --> 19:58.230
In terms of term limits, I don't have a strong view on that.

19:58.230 --> 20:02.320
Different countries use different approaches.

20:02.320 --> 20:05.520
Of course, the President always has the option to reappoint

20:05.520 --> 20:10.300
or not reappoint Fed Chairmen, and the Senate always has the option

20:10.300 --> 20:12.310
of confirming or not confirming.

20:12.310 --> 20:16.930
So, in that respect, term limits are redundant.

20:16.930 --> 20:21.000
And, indeed, if you had term limits on the Fed Chairman, that would be, I think,

20:21.000 --> 20:24.100
the only office in the federal government besides President and Vice President

20:24.100 --> 20:25.880
that would have that restriction.

20:25.880 --> 20:30.040
But that being said, again, I don't view this as a major issue,

20:30.040 --> 20:35.810
nor have I seen it actively discussed in, I think, in the Hill.

20:35.810 --> 20:37.430
Perhaps I missed it.

20:37.430 --> 20:41.650
But I don't have a strong view about that.

20:41.650 --> 20:44.640
My 10-year term, of course, is not as Chairman.

20:44.640 --> 20:50.890
My 10-year remaining term is as a Governor, so that's not relevant, really,

20:50.890 --> 21:00.170
to the question that you asked about.

21:00.170 --> 21:00.760
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

21:00.760 --> 21:02.130
Binya Appelbaum, the New York Times.

21:02.130 --> 21:04.090
I want to go back to asset purchases.

21:04.090 --> 21:06.660
You've spoken a lot about the power of forward guidance,

21:06.660 --> 21:08.560
the power of clear communication.

21:08.560 --> 21:10.150
I understand that it's a complicated issue,

21:10.150 --> 21:13.750
but why would you leave on the table the additional power of saying to markets,

21:13.750 --> 21:16.070
"We're going to keep doing this for a while"?

21:16.070 --> 21:18.260
And, separately, there's been a lot of conversation

21:18.260 --> 21:21.100
about the risks associated with quantitative easing.

21:21.100 --> 21:23.280
I wonder if you could tell us, when is the last time you spoke

21:23.280 --> 21:24.810
with someone who is unemployed?

21:24.810 --> 21:27.730
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

21:27.730 --> 21:28.610
Pretty recently.

21:28.610 --> 21:30.410
I have a relative that is unemployed.

21:30.410 --> 21:35.660
But I come from a small town in South Carolina

21:35.660 --> 21:40.000
that has taken a big hit from the recession.

21:40.000 --> 21:44.050
Last time I was there, the unemployment rate was about 15 percent.

21:44.050 --> 21:45.030
I think it's better now.

21:45.030 --> 21:48.740
The home that I was raised in had just been foreclosed upon

21:48.740 --> 21:50.800
when I was visiting there.

21:50.800 --> 22:00.000
I have great concern about the unemployed both for their own sake,

22:00.000 --> 22:02.850
but also because the loss of skills,

22:02.850 --> 22:06.730
the loss of labor force attachment is bad for our whole economy.

22:06.730 --> 22:08.820
It reduces tax revenues.

22:08.820 --> 22:11.750
It reduces productivity.

22:11.750 --> 22:19.200
So I think it's very, very important that we act to address unemployment,

22:19.200 --> 22:23.050
and I think the Federal Reserve-I think most people would agree-the Federal

22:23.050 --> 22:27.060
Reserve has been fairly active in that regard.

22:27.060 --> 22:30.660
In terms of costs, there are a number of different costs,

22:30.660 --> 22:32.640
and I mentioned some of them in my remarks.

22:32.640 --> 22:37.600
I think one that has recently been discussed-Governor Stein brought it

22:37.600 --> 22:40.920
up in a speech-is the issue of financial stability.

22:40.920 --> 22:45.690
Clearly, financial instability, if it were allowed to be sufficiently serious,

22:45.690 --> 22:49.400
would be a threat to employment, a threat to jobs, and a threat to production.

22:49.400 --> 22:51.780
So, obviously, given the experience of the past few years,

22:51.780 --> 22:56.350
we want to be sure that we're not unnecessarily encouraging excessive

22:56.350 --> 22:59.150
risk-taking or other problems in the financial markets.

22:59.150 --> 23:02.680
We do address that through a number of means,

23:02.680 --> 23:06.480
including monitoring the financial system, regulation, supervision,

23:06.480 --> 23:08.210
communication, and the like.

23:08.210 --> 23:10.990
But this is a potential concern that a number

23:10.990 --> 23:14.050
of my colleagues are worried about, and it's one of the things we talked

23:14.050 --> 23:18.370
about in our discussion of the costs and risks of balance sheet policies.

23:18.370 --> 23:18.820
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

23:18.820 --> 23:20.240
Then why would you leave forward guidance on the table?

23:20.240 --> 23:20.660
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

23:20.660 --> 23:22.540
I don't know what you mean by "on the table."

23:22.540 --> 23:23.000
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

23:23.000 --> 23:25.400
Why not use it as a tool to increase the power of asset purchases?

23:25.400 --> 23:26.830
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

23:26.830 --> 23:31.810
Well, again, I don't-we've not been able to come to an agreement

23:31.810 --> 23:33.970
about what guidance we should give.

23:33.970 --> 23:37.680
And part of the concern is-is that as we go forward, we-you know,

23:37.680 --> 23:41.680
we'll have to factor in the efficacy, which is another issue.

23:41.680 --> 23:47.580
I mean, there's a wide range of views about how effective asset purchases are

23:47.580 --> 23:49.440
in terms of moving the economy.

23:49.440 --> 23:51.770
So, as we move forward in time,

23:51.770 --> 23:54.690
we'll be learning about how effective the policy is,

23:54.690 --> 23:57.800
and what costs and risks there may be associated with it.

23:57.800 --> 24:04.530
And, as we do that, perhaps we will be able to give more-explicit guidance.

24:04.530 --> 24:07.230
And I agree with you 100 percent that that would be more effective

24:07.230 --> 24:10.550
if we could give numerical guidance.

24:10.550 --> 24:13.350
But, you know, I think the Federal Reserve has come a long way.

24:13.350 --> 24:16.030
You know, in 1994, we didn't even tell people

24:16.030 --> 24:17.960
when we changed the federal funds rate.

24:17.960 --> 24:20.390
Now, we're telling you when-you know, what the state of the economy is going

24:20.390 --> 24:22.660
to be when we raise the federal funds rate.

24:22.660 --> 24:26.320
So, we are making progress in terms of forward guidance.

24:26.320 --> 24:34.050
DONNA BORAK.

24:34.050 --> 24:36.320
Chairman, Donna Borak with American Banker.

24:36.320 --> 24:40.270
There have been a number of policies that have been floated

24:40.270 --> 24:44.420
in the last few months regarding of strengthening U.S. banks,

24:44.420 --> 24:47.200
one being increased-perhaps making adjustments

24:47.200 --> 24:49.490
to the Basel III's leverage ratio,

24:49.490 --> 24:54.390
requiring banks to hold long-term unsecured debt in the event of a resolution,

24:54.390 --> 24:59.350
and then, also, placing a cap on banks' nondeposit liabilities.

24:59.350 --> 25:02.740
Firstly, is-do you agree with these policies and whether

25:02.740 --> 25:05.750
or not the Fed should be pursuing them along with supervisors?

25:05.750 --> 25:09.400
And, secondly, in the context of this question of too-big-to-fail, I mean,

25:09.400 --> 25:12.710
do you think these policies would actually help to finally convince the market

25:12.710 --> 25:15.540
and the public that too-big-to-fail doesn't exist?

25:15.540 --> 25:16.870
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

25:16.870 --> 25:23.890
Well, I think capital is an important element in addressing too-big-to-fail.

25:23.890 --> 25:29.840
One of the things that will be proposed, and is not in effect yet,

25:29.840 --> 25:33.290
will be surcharges on the largest banks-that is,

25:33.290 --> 25:37.290
the largest financial institutions will have to hold more capital as a percent

25:37.290 --> 25:41.250
of their risk-weighted assets than smaller banks do.

25:41.250 --> 25:44.220
That will increase their cost of funding and, to some extent,

25:44.220 --> 25:50.370
will both equalize their cost of funding with other banks and make them safer

25:50.370 --> 25:53.930
so that the risk of their failure is limited.

25:53.930 --> 25:55.580
So I think that's an important step.

25:55.580 --> 25:59.870
And there are many other restrictions in both Dodd-Frank and Basel,

25:59.870 --> 26:02.350
including liquidity restrictions and so on,

26:02.350 --> 26:09.940
that apply most strictly to the largest institutions.

26:09.940 --> 26:13.940
In terms of the financing, it is true, I think,

26:13.940 --> 26:20.720
that excessive reliance on short-term uninsured funding does present some risks.

26:20.720 --> 26:22.830
And there are different ways to address that.

26:22.830 --> 26:26.630
But one way to address it is through liquidity regulation.

26:26.630 --> 26:32.590
And both in our 165-166 rule and now through Basel III,

26:32.590 --> 26:38.750
we will be putting forward restrictions on the kinds of financing,

26:38.750 --> 26:46.740
the limits on how much illiquidity and liquidity risk firms can take.

26:46.740 --> 26:51.900
Again, I don't think too-big-to-fail is solved now.

26:51.900 --> 26:56.270
We're doing a number of things, which I think will help.

26:56.270 --> 26:59.500
We need to keep assessing that, and we'll be able to tell by looking

26:59.500 --> 27:06.730
at market indicators, by doing our own stress tests and the like.

27:06.730 --> 27:10.860
And if we don't, you know, if we don't achieve the goal,

27:10.860 --> 27:12.540
I think we'll have to do additional steps.

27:12.540 --> 27:13.330
It is important.

27:13.330 --> 27:15.710
I mean, it's not something we can just forget about.

27:15.710 --> 27:20.980
It may take some time, but too-big-to-fail was a major part

27:20.980 --> 27:22.940
of the source of the crisis.

27:22.940 --> 27:26.680
And we will not have successfully responded to the crisis

27:26.680 --> 27:30.340
if we don't address that problem successfully.

27:30.340 --> 27:33.340
JEFF KEARNS.

27:35.850 --> 27:37.560
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

27:37.560 --> 27:39.320
Jeff Kearns from Bloomberg.

27:39.320 --> 27:43.410
Looking at the level for credit scores for new home loans rising

27:43.410 --> 27:46.010
into the 700s-is that something you would consider

27:46.010 --> 27:51.520
to be a successful transmission of monetary policy,

27:51.520 --> 27:59.970
and the-especially given how well the Fed says that banks are capitalized?

28:01.400 --> 28:03.210
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

28:03.210 --> 28:06.430
Well, the tightening of credit in mortgage markets-I mean,

28:06.430 --> 28:10.380
our sense is that it's gone too far.

28:10.380 --> 28:14.460
I mean, some tightening was obviously needed.

28:14.460 --> 28:18.550
There were people who bought houses prior to the crisis

28:18.550 --> 28:21.530
who really couldn't sustain a mortgage.

28:21.530 --> 28:24.370
So, terms and conditions have been tightened up,

28:24.370 --> 28:31.120
and we're now seeing much higher credit quality requirements

28:31.120 --> 28:32.680
on potential borrowers.

28:32.680 --> 28:37.830
We are concerned that a variety of factors,

28:37.830 --> 28:41.650
such as concerns about putbacks the banks may have,

28:41.650 --> 28:46.110
uncertainties about regulation-which we're working on to get done as quickly

28:46.110 --> 28:51.400
as possible-may have tightened the mortgage credit box more

28:51.400 --> 28:55.470
than would be desirable in a long-run healthy economy.

28:55.470 --> 28:58.710
That does have some effect on monetary policy.

28:58.710 --> 29:02.780
One of the most powerful tools we have is bringing down mortgage rates

29:02.780 --> 29:07.050
and stimulating homebuying, construction, and related industries.

29:07.050 --> 29:09.510
So that is an issue that we take into account.

29:09.510 --> 29:16.250
I would say one thing, which is that as the housing industry has strengthened

29:16.250 --> 29:19.870
and home prices have gone up, that has actually brought some people

29:19.870 --> 29:24.980
into the credit box in the sense that the number of people, for example,

29:24.980 --> 29:30.790
who are underwater on their mortgages is declining as house prices go up.

29:30.790 --> 29:33.740
So, as people have bigger down payments, bigger equity in their homes,

29:33.740 --> 29:35.660
they become more creditworthy.

29:35.660 --> 29:39.550
So, to some extent, not-I don't want to overstate it-but to some extent,

29:39.550 --> 29:43.580
monetary policy-by strengthening the housing market,

29:43.580 --> 29:48.860
helping support house prices-is bringing more people into the mortgage market.

29:48.860 --> 29:53.070
PETER BARNES.

29:53.070 --> 29:55.010
Peter Barnes of Fox Business, sir.

29:55.010 --> 29:58.050
The stock market has been hitting all-time highs.

29:58.050 --> 30:02.810
It's recovered all of its losses from the financial crisis.

30:02.810 --> 30:08.300
I just want to know if I-from you, if I still have time to get in.

30:08.300 --> 30:12.590
But seriously, how do you feel about that?

30:12.590 --> 30:13.650
Is it good?

30:13.650 --> 30:14.390
Is it bad?

30:14.390 --> 30:16.100
Mission accomplished?

30:16.100 --> 30:19.820
And are you worried about bubbles?

30:19.820 --> 30:23.560
We're still at 7.7 percent unemployment.

30:23.560 --> 30:25.400
I mean, is the-what do you think?

30:25.400 --> 30:26.280
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

30:26.280 --> 30:27.490
That's right.

30:27.490 --> 30:30.130
We're not targeting asset prices.

30:30.130 --> 30:32.480
We're not measuring success in terms of the stock market.

30:32.480 --> 30:36.230
We're measuring success in terms of our mandate, which is employment

30:36.230 --> 30:40.230
and price stability, and that's what we're trying to achieve.

30:40.230 --> 30:43.570
We do monitor the entire financial system,

30:43.570 --> 30:46.310
not just the parts that we supervise or regulate.

30:46.310 --> 30:49.780
It includes the stock market and other asset markets.

30:49.780 --> 30:51.430
We use a variety of metrics.

30:51.430 --> 30:55.730
And I don't want to, now, be pulled into going

30:55.730 --> 30:59.760
through every individual financial market and assessing it.

30:59.760 --> 31:04.610
But, in the stock market, you know, we don't see at this point anything that's

31:04.610 --> 31:07.950
out of line with historical patterns.

31:07.950 --> 31:10.610
In particular, you should remember, of course,

31:10.610 --> 31:14.660
that while the Dow may be hitting a high,

31:14.660 --> 31:17.210
it's in nominal terms, it's not in real terms.

31:17.210 --> 31:21.210
And if you adjust for inflation and for the growth of the economy, you know,

31:21.210 --> 31:23.670
we're still some distance from the high.

31:23.670 --> 31:27.720
I don't think it's all that surprising that the stock market would rise,

31:27.720 --> 31:35.130
given that there has been increased optimism about the economy and the share

31:35.130 --> 31:37.620
of income going to profits has been very high.

31:37.620 --> 31:40.330
Profit increases have been substantial,

31:40.330 --> 31:41.850
and the relationship between stock prices

31:41.850 --> 31:46.500
and earnings is not particularly unusual at this point.

31:46.500 --> 31:50.050
MARCY GORDON.

31:51.490 --> 31:53.570
Thank you.

31:53.570 --> 31:55.870
Marcy Gordon with the Associated Press.

31:55.870 --> 31:57.680
Mr. Chairman, the statement mentions

31:57.680 --> 32:01.300
that fiscal policy has become more restrictive.

32:01.300 --> 32:08.140
How much of a drag on growth do you see from the Social Security tax increase

32:08.140 --> 32:13.440
and the across-the-board spending cuts that went into effect on March 1?

32:13.440 --> 32:18.120
And is it possible that the Fed might see a need to provide more support

32:18.120 --> 32:24.870
to the economy if that-because of that drag, the drag on fiscal?

32:24.870 --> 32:26.060
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

32:26.060 --> 32:29.530
Well, our analysis is fairly comparable to analysis

32:29.530 --> 32:32.400
that the Congressional Budget Office has presented to the Congress.

32:32.400 --> 32:37.510
And they estimate that putting together all the fiscal measures,

32:37.510 --> 32:41.770
including the fiscal cliff deal, the sequester, and other cuts,

32:41.770 --> 32:46.750
that federal fiscal restraint in 2013 is cutting something

32:46.750 --> 32:53.710
like 1½ percentage points off of growth, which, of course, is very significant.

32:53.710 --> 32:56.710
So, that is an issue for us.

32:56.710 --> 33:02.710
We-you know, we take as given what the fiscal authorities are doing.

33:02.710 --> 33:04.740
The economy is weaker.

33:04.740 --> 33:07.510
Job creation is slower than it would be otherwise.

33:07.510 --> 33:09.070
And so, that is one of the reasons

33:09.070 --> 33:13.280
that our policy has been as aggressive as it is.

33:13.280 --> 33:15.960
That being said, as I've said many times,

33:15.960 --> 33:21.320
monetary policy cannot offset a fiscal restraint of that magnitude,

33:21.320 --> 33:25.380
and so the final outcome will be worse-or,

33:25.380 --> 33:30.900
in terms of jobs-than would have been the case with less fiscal restraint.

33:30.900 --> 33:34.670
I want to emphasize that I do believe

33:34.670 --> 33:38.820
that long-term fiscal stability is extremely important.

33:38.820 --> 33:41.020
And I urge Congress and the Administration, as I always do,

33:41.020 --> 33:44.230
when I go to testify to do whatever is necessary to put us

33:44.230 --> 33:47.340
on a sustainable fiscal path going forward.

33:47.340 --> 33:52.780
But, in doing so, I think it's a good idea to pay attention to the impact

33:52.780 --> 33:59.200
in the near term on what is still not a completely satisfactory recovery.

33:59.200 --> 34:02.250
STEVE BECKNER.

34:04.510 --> 34:06.770
Mr. Chairman, good afternoon.

34:06.770 --> 34:10.210
You earlier stressed that you want to see improvement

34:10.210 --> 34:15.080
in the labor market sustained, and that to make that determination,

34:15.080 --> 34:16.920
you have to have adequate economic growth,

34:16.920 --> 34:22.760
and yet the projections in the revised Summary of Economic Projections has kind

34:22.760 --> 34:26.010
of a-it seems like an Okun's law dilemma there.

34:26.010 --> 34:31.020
You-they have reduced the projected rate of unemployment, but, at the same time,

34:31.020 --> 34:32.850
lowered the growth forecast.

34:32.850 --> 34:34.750
So how do you square those two?

34:34.750 --> 34:37.810
How do you get sustained improvement in the labor market

34:37.810 --> 34:39.680
if the economy is going to slow down?

34:39.680 --> 34:40.820
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

34:40.820 --> 34:44.340
Well, if in fact that happens, it's an issue, obviously.

34:44.340 --> 34:48.680
There's been some disconnect, at least in the short run,

34:48.680 --> 34:52.810
between unemployment rate changes and growth during this recovery,

34:52.810 --> 34:54.390
and there've been periods, at least,

34:54.390 --> 34:59.480
where unemployment has fallen relatively quickly even though growth has been

34:59.480 --> 34:59.990
more limited.

34:59.990 --> 35:02.580
So we're just going to have to monitor developments

35:02.580 --> 35:05.900
in the economy and see what happens.

35:05.900 --> 35:10.620
You're right that we're not forecasting extraordinarily strong growth.

35:10.620 --> 35:13.630
But it is also true, as you-I think, you noted,

35:13.630 --> 35:21.150
that our projections for unemployment in the fourth quarter are noticeably lower

35:21.150 --> 35:25.420
than they were in September when we first announced this asset purchase program.

35:25.420 --> 35:29.680
So there has been some improvement in the outlook, as measured by that metric.

35:29.680 --> 35:31.420
But you're right.

35:31.420 --> 35:35.170
You know, we do need to see a sustained improvement.

35:35.170 --> 35:39.180
One month, two months doesn't cut it.

35:39.180 --> 35:44.460
And, normally, you would expect that you would need to see a reasonable pace

35:44.460 --> 35:46.270
of GDP growth in order to achieve that.

35:46.270 --> 35:50.680
So we're just going to have to keep providing support for the economy

35:50.680 --> 35:54.180
and see-you know, see how things evolve.

35:54.180 --> 35:58.900
VICTORIA McGRANE.

35:58.900 --> 35:59.430
Hi, Chairman.

35:59.430 --> 36:02.100
Victoria McGrane with the Dow Jones Newswires.

36:02.100 --> 36:05.670
In the stress test that the Fed recently conducted,

36:05.670 --> 36:09.420
there were an adverse scenario and a severely adverse scenario,

36:09.420 --> 36:12.970
and you published results of the-from the individual banks on-under the

36:12.970 --> 36:14.860
adverse-severely adverse scenario.

36:14.860 --> 36:15.510
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

36:15.510 --> 36:16.760
Right. VICTORIA McGRANE.

36:16.760 --> 36:19.800
But you didn't under the merely adverse scenario,

36:19.800 --> 36:24.320
which featured an inflation shock followed by a quick rise in short-term rates.

36:24.320 --> 36:26.280
So, I have a two-part question.

36:26.280 --> 36:30.210
First, why didn't the Fed publish those results?

36:30.210 --> 36:33.840
And, second, even if you can't share how individual banks performed,

36:33.840 --> 36:37.540
what did you learn from how-from the results you did see

36:37.540 --> 36:39.830
under that adverse scenario?

36:39.830 --> 36:42.190
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

36:42.190 --> 36:45.260
Well, the reason for publishing the severely adverse scenario, of course,

36:45.260 --> 36:47.830
is that's the ultimate acid test, you know,

36:47.830 --> 36:51.190
whether the banks are sufficiently capitalized.

36:51.190 --> 36:54.680
Presumably, if they can survive a severely adverse scenario,

36:54.680 --> 37:01.420
then an adverse scenario would-obviously less strenuous, less stressful,

37:01.420 --> 37:05.750
and they wouldn't have as much difficulty.

37:05.750 --> 37:09.050
So, I think, in terms of evaluating the health of the banks,

37:09.050 --> 37:11.690
the severely adverse scenario is the right one.

37:11.690 --> 37:14.970
That being said, I don't see any principle reason why we couldn't provide

37:14.970 --> 37:16.360
that information.

37:16.360 --> 37:21.740
I will find out at some point why we are doing it that way.

37:21.740 --> 37:27.090
The severely adverse scenario is mostly just a scaling-up

37:27.090 --> 37:28.870
of the adverse scenario, for the most part.

37:28.870 --> 37:29.800
There are some differences.

37:29.800 --> 37:36.070
For example, we have used some of our work to look at interest rate risk

37:36.070 --> 37:39.410
and interest rate sensitivity and, you know,

37:39.410 --> 37:45.710
found generally that banks can also sustain a significant increase

37:45.710 --> 37:48.410
in long-term interest rates as well for a number of reasons,

37:48.410 --> 37:52.300
one of them being that higher interest rates increase their franchise value

37:52.300 --> 37:56.480
because it increases their net interest margin over time.

37:56.480 --> 38:02.080
So, again, I think the severely adverse scenario is the one

38:02.080 --> 38:04.470
that really puts them to the test.

38:04.470 --> 38:08.930
But we're always talking about, you know, what information will be useful

38:08.930 --> 38:17.320
to investors and to the press.

38:17.320 --> 38:17.820
RYAN AVENT.

38:17.820 --> 38:20.680
Ryan Avent, The Economist.

38:20.680 --> 38:25.520
You've noted that most of the Committee members don't expect an increase

38:25.520 --> 38:29.770
in rates until 2015 or 2016, and it looks, in the projections,

38:29.770 --> 38:34.300
as though the expectation for the long-run rate of the federal funds target is

38:34.300 --> 38:37.210
around 4 percent, which would be below the sort

38:37.210 --> 38:39.470
of peak rate we saw before the recession.

38:39.470 --> 38:42.550
Given the Committee's concerns about unconventional policy,

38:42.550 --> 38:45.220
is there any feeling on the Committee

38:45.220 --> 38:47.390
that perhaps recovery isn't going fast enough,

38:47.390 --> 38:49.640
and that more accommodation would be justified?

38:49.640 --> 38:52.420
And has there been any discussion about a change in policy targets to try

38:52.420 --> 38:55.140
to stay effective without much of a cushion there

38:55.140 --> 38:58.680
between the fed funds target rate and the zero lower bound?

38:58.680 --> 39:02.340
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

39:02.340 --> 39:05.840
Well, as you point out, we're at the zero lower bound,

39:05.840 --> 39:09.910
and that makes further accommodation not impossible but more difficult

39:09.910 --> 39:14.370
and harder to predict, and with more side effects that are difficult to predict.

39:14.370 --> 39:18.850
I'm not sure I understand the whole thrust of your question.

39:18.850 --> 39:25.750
We have-as you know, we have given this guideline for-so we call them signposts

39:25.750 --> 39:29.630
for how the funds rate is going to evolve over time.

39:29.630 --> 39:32.580
And, as a lot of academic research shows, you know,

39:32.580 --> 39:36.170
when you're close to the zero lower bound, by telling markets that you're going

39:36.170 --> 39:38.860
to keep rates low for a significant period,

39:38.860 --> 39:40.850
that's one way to get longer-term rates down

39:40.850 --> 39:42.460
and to provide more stimulus to the economy.

39:42.460 --> 39:46.060
And we think this has been a pretty effective tool.

39:47.080 --> 39:49.920
Now, we could go further.

39:49.920 --> 39:57.080
We could lower even further, say, the unemployment rate number that we hit.

39:57.080 --> 39:59.020
We've discussed variants, and at least one member

39:59.020 --> 40:00.940
of the Committee has suggested that.

40:00.940 --> 40:07.830
But for right now, we find that the thresholds that we have put

40:07.830 --> 40:12.040
into that rate guidance seem to be sufficient

40:12.040 --> 40:17.330
to approximate the-what's called the optimal control path of interest rates,

40:17.330 --> 40:21.820
that it seems to give a path of unemployment and inflation that's about as good

40:21.820 --> 40:24.250
as we can get with the monetary policy tools that we have.

40:24.250 --> 40:26.200
It doesn't mean we're satisfied.

40:26.200 --> 40:30.150
It just means that we don't have enough fire power to get the economy back

40:30.150 --> 40:32.760
to full employment more quickly.

40:32.760 --> 40:35.750
I don't know if that was responsive or not.

40:35.750 --> 40:36.280
RYAN AVENT.

40:36.280 --> 40:39.860
I guess I'm not-given the concerns about unconventional policy relative

40:39.860 --> 40:43.750
to normal interest rate policy, is there a feeling that more should be done

40:43.750 --> 40:46.270
so that when the next potential recession rolls around,

40:46.270 --> 40:48.040
we have more room to cut rates,

40:48.040 --> 40:51.130
or are you comfortable just using these threshold policies on an ongoing basis?

40:51.130 --> 40:51.650
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

40:51.650 --> 40:54.180
I see. So you're talking about the inflation target, basically.

40:54.180 --> 40:55.010
Is that fair?

40:55.010 --> 40:55.730
RYAN AVENT.

40:55.730 --> 40:57.620
Yes, I think so.

40:57.620 --> 40:58.250
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

40:58.250 --> 41:00.050
Yes, okay.

41:00.050 --> 41:04.370
So, historically, the argument for having inflation greater

41:04.370 --> 41:06.930
than zero-we define price stability as 2 percent inflation,

41:06.930 --> 41:10.180
as do most central banks around the world.

41:10.180 --> 41:13.330
And one might ask, "Well, price stability should be zero inflation.

41:13.330 --> 41:15.890
Why do you choose 2 percent instead of zero?"

41:15.890 --> 41:17.820
And the answer is, the question you're raising,

41:17.820 --> 41:21.040
which is that if you have zero inflation,

41:21.040 --> 41:24.500
you're very close to the deflation zone, and nominal interest rates will be

41:24.500 --> 41:28.140
so low that it would be very difficult to respond fully to recessions.

41:28.140 --> 41:30.690
And so, historical experience has suggested

41:30.690 --> 41:34.680
that 2 percent is an appropriate balance between the cost of inflation

41:34.680 --> 41:36.840
and the cost that you're referring to.

41:36.840 --> 41:38.530
We haven't contemplated changing that.

41:38.530 --> 41:41.380
We just put that number in, as you know, fairly recently.

41:41.380 --> 41:43.500
I think at this point, it's still being debated

41:43.500 --> 41:45.560
in academic circles that-you know,

41:45.560 --> 41:47.800
and we'll see what kind of outcome they come up with.

41:47.800 --> 41:50.800
But it's an interesting question to try to quantify.

41:50.800 --> 41:53.060
There is research, for example, which asks the question,

41:53.060 --> 41:56.480
how often do you tend to hit the zero lower bound?

41:56.480 --> 41:59.920
And our belief a few years ago was that it was a very rare event,

41:59.920 --> 42:01.790
and now it's become more common.

42:01.790 --> 42:03.920
So I'm sure there'll be a lot of thinking about this

42:03.920 --> 42:06.980
in academic and other circles.

42:06.980 --> 42:11.660
BEN WEYL. Hi, Mr. Chairman.

42:11.660 --> 42:13.430
Ben Weyl, Congressional Quarterly.

42:13.430 --> 42:17.020
There's a lot of talk about whether certain institutions are too big to fail.

42:17.020 --> 42:19.340
And I wanted to get at a different, if related, question.

42:19.340 --> 42:23.620
In 1980, let's say, there was about-the financial sector comprised

42:23.620 --> 42:29.090
about 5 percent of the U.S. economy, U.S. GDP; now it's about 9 percent.

42:29.090 --> 42:33.400
And I'm wondering if you think that shift is beneficial to the U.S. economy?

42:33.400 --> 42:35.430
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

42:35.430 --> 42:38.500
I don't think I know the answer to that question.

42:38.500 --> 42:43.940
Certainly, the financial system has-I could argue two ways.

42:43.940 --> 42:46.500
I could say, well, the U.S. economy grew pretty well

42:46.500 --> 42:51.440
between 1945 and 1975 or 1980.

42:51.440 --> 42:56.570
And the financial system was much simpler and didn't have a lot

42:56.570 --> 43:00.350
of exotic derivatives and so on.

43:00.350 --> 43:04.270
So that would be-that would be one way to argue that maybe, you know,

43:04.270 --> 43:08.110
all of this extra financial activity is not justified.

43:08.110 --> 43:11.590
On the other hand, the world is a lot more complicated.

43:11.590 --> 43:13.470
We're a lot-the world is a lot more international.

43:13.470 --> 43:20.240
You have large multinational firms that are connecting resources, savers,

43:20.240 --> 43:22.190
and investors in different countries.

43:22.190 --> 43:26.780
There's a lot more demand for risk sharing, for liquidity services, and so on.

43:26.780 --> 43:31.200
So, I think, based on that and based on the innovations

43:31.200 --> 43:34.130
that information technology have created in lots of industries,

43:34.130 --> 43:37.830
you would expect financial services to be somewhat bigger.

43:37.830 --> 43:39.680
So I don't really know the answer to that question.

43:39.680 --> 43:44.550
I think that my predecessor Paul Volcker's claim that the only contribution

43:44.550 --> 43:47.960
to the financial industry is the automatic teller machine might be a

43:47.960 --> 43:49.360
little exaggerated.

43:49.360 --> 43:51.250
I know that people-some people have that view.

43:51.250 --> 43:54.080
Again, I don't know the answer.

43:54.080 --> 43:58.000
I think that a somewhat bigger financial sector can be justified

43:58.000 --> 44:00.690
by the wider range of services

44:00.690 --> 44:04.180
and the more globalized financial economic system that we have.

44:04.180 --> 44:07.110
But the exact number, I can't really say.

44:07.110 --> 44:09.980
JEREMY TORDJMAN.

44:09.980 --> 44:14.600
Hi, I'm Jeremy Tordjman with the AFP Newswire Agency.

44:14.600 --> 44:17.710
I have a follow-up question on Cyprus.

44:17.710 --> 44:22.130
As a central banker, do you think it was either appropriate

44:22.130 --> 44:27.170
or fair to impose a levy on every bank deposit in Cyprus,

44:27.170 --> 44:30.740
even those insured by the European Union itself?

44:30.740 --> 44:31.580
Thank you.

44:31.580 --> 44:33.350
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

44:33.350 --> 44:36.160
Well, I have not been involved in those conversations

44:36.160 --> 44:39.530
and I don't necessarily know all the details, and so I know they're grappling

44:39.530 --> 44:42.480
with a very difficult problem.

44:42.480 --> 44:48.160
I think the issue they face is that there's a pretty big hole, a financial hole,

44:48.160 --> 44:54.320
in the sense that there is a fiscal issue

44:54.320 --> 44:58.630
and there's also a bank restructuring recapitalization issue.

44:58.630 --> 45:04.000
So, they're looking for resources where they can find them.

45:04.000 --> 45:06.740
I think everyone understands that there are certain risks

45:06.740 --> 45:12.700
with that besides the equity issue of taxing lower-income people.

45:12.700 --> 45:17.740
There is the issue of setting a precedent that might reduce confidence

45:17.740 --> 45:20.870
in banks in subsequent periods.

45:20.870 --> 45:24.250
But again, that being said, it's a very tough issue.

45:24.250 --> 45:29.480
And I-and finding the resources to solve Cyprus's problem, you know,

45:29.480 --> 45:31.470
there's probably no easy way to do it.

45:31.470 --> 45:36.790
And we're going to keep monitoring that.

45:36.790 --> 45:40.610
But I don't envy them that particular challenge.

45:40.610 --> 45:43.610
GREG ROBB.

45:43.610 --> 45:44.970
Thank you.

45:44.970 --> 45:50.600
There's been a trend in the last couple of years where the economy kind

45:50.600 --> 45:55.720
of jumped out of the gate in the first part of the year only to kind of falter.

45:55.720 --> 45:57.620
Is that something that you're worried about this year?

45:57.620 --> 46:01.330
And does that suggest that QE might have to stay kind

46:01.330 --> 46:04.170
of at the same pace you are now and some into the third quarter

46:04.170 --> 46:05.970
until we're sure about that-that trend?

46:05.970 --> 46:06.390
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

46:06.390 --> 46:09.760
Well, you're absolutely right that there's been a certain tendency

46:09.760 --> 46:13.720
for a spring slump that we've seen a few times.

46:13.720 --> 46:17.280
One possible explanation for that-besides some freaky things,

46:17.280 --> 46:22.770
some weather events and so on, one possible explanation is seasonality.

46:22.770 --> 46:30.710
Because of the severity of the recession in 2007 to 2009,

46:30.710 --> 46:34.020
the seasonals got distorted.

46:34.020 --> 46:42.290
And they may have led-and I say "may" because the statistical experts-many

46:42.290 --> 46:46.590
of them deny it, but it's possible that they led job creation

46:46.590 --> 46:51.450
and GDP to be exaggerated to some extent early in the year.

46:51.450 --> 46:55.020
Our assessment is, though, that at this point,

46:55.020 --> 46:59.930
that we're far enough away from the recession that those seasonal factors ought

46:59.930 --> 47:02.750
to be pretty much washing out by now.

47:02.750 --> 47:05.150
So if we do, in fact, see a slump,

47:05.150 --> 47:08.630
it would probably be due to real fundamental causes

47:08.630 --> 47:11.080
and then we would obviously have to respond to that.

47:11.080 --> 47:16.600
As I said, we're planning to adjust our tools to respond to changes

47:16.600 --> 47:21.350
in the outlook, and that can go either direction.

47:21.350 --> 47:23.540
DONALD JUDD.

47:24.790 --> 47:28.760
Donald Judd, CBS News.

47:28.760 --> 47:30.960
I was wondering if you could tell me how,

47:30.960 --> 47:35.220
if a run on the banks happens in Cyprus, how that might affect U.S. markets.

47:35.220 --> 47:39.840
And also, is it possible for the U.S. to levy a tax

47:39.840 --> 47:42.340
on regular deposits here, or why not?

47:42.340 --> 47:45.010
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

47:45.010 --> 47:49.750
Well, as someone mentioned, Cyprus is a tiny economy,

47:49.750 --> 47:57.190
and I don't think that these issues, as worrisome as they are and as concerned

47:57.190 --> 47:59.610
as we would be for the Cypriote people,

47:59.610 --> 48:04.590
I don't think that they have direct implications for the U.S. economy.

48:04.590 --> 48:08.630
The only way that they would create a problem would be

48:08.630 --> 48:12.950
if the runs became contagious in some sense,

48:12.950 --> 48:15.710
if depositors in other countries lost confidence.

48:15.710 --> 48:17.900
But at this point, I'm not aware of any evidence

48:17.900 --> 48:20.180
that that is, in fact, the case.

48:20.180 --> 48:21.820
The argument the Europeans are making is

48:21.820 --> 48:26.180
that Cyprus is a very unique situation, very different situation.

48:26.180 --> 48:31.040
And, indeed, it is quite unusual to have a banking sector as large

48:31.040 --> 48:33.420
as they have relative to their economy.

48:33.420 --> 48:39.320
In terms of the United States, you know, the FDIC was founded, I think, in 1934,

48:39.320 --> 48:43.060
and insured deposits, and they're very proud of the fact

48:43.060 --> 48:46.070
that no one's ever lost a dime in insured deposits.

48:46.070 --> 48:50.540
And, during the crisis, the response of the government was, in fact,

48:50.540 --> 48:56.310
to increase the level of, you know, depositor account sizes that were insured.

48:56.310 --> 49:02.630
So I consider that to be extremely unlikely in the United States.

49:02.630 --> 49:07.740
DON LEE. Don Lee at the L.A. Times.

49:07.740 --> 49:13.680
Would you be in favor of reducing the flow of stimulus if we had another month

49:13.680 --> 49:19.010
or two, as we did in February, of job growth and the unemployment rate dropping,

49:19.010 --> 49:22.390
but the long-term unemployed didn't change much?

49:22.390 --> 49:26.580
And, on a related question, how much of a pickup do you expect to see

49:26.580 --> 49:31.850
in the labor force participation rate, and what will we need to see there

49:31.850 --> 49:34.960
for that to show substantial improvement?

49:34.960 --> 49:35.800
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

49:35.800 --> 49:39.830
On the first question, as I said, I mean that's a decision for the Committee.

49:39.830 --> 49:41.420
We're going to have to make a judgment

49:41.420 --> 49:45.310
about how significant the improvement is, how sustained it is.

49:45.310 --> 49:53.530
Long-term unemployment is one dimension of the unemployment problem.

49:53.530 --> 49:56.550
But I think that probably the best way to get the long-term unemployed back

49:56.550 --> 49:59.730
to work is to get an overall strong labor market.

49:59.730 --> 50:05.150
And I think that's-we'd be looking at the overall key indicators

50:05.150 --> 50:09.880
like overall unemployment rate, payrolls and hiring,

50:09.880 --> 50:12.580
and some of the other things that I mentioned.

50:12.580 --> 50:16.530
The other part of your question was about- DON LEE.

50:16.530 --> 50:17.190
Labor force.

50:17.190 --> 50:17.590
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

50:17.590 --> 50:19.240
Labor force participation, yes.

50:19.240 --> 50:23.900
Labor force participation has been declining on a trend-like basis

50:23.900 --> 50:26.370
in the United States for a while.

50:26.370 --> 50:30.510
That's the result, mostly, of demographic factors,

50:30.510 --> 50:33.310
partly the aging of the population,

50:33.310 --> 50:40.010
partly the fact that female participation is no longer increasing-it's

50:40.010 --> 50:41.310
in fact decreasing a little bit.

50:41.310 --> 50:46.100
It's also the case that labor force attachment within people

50:46.100 --> 50:49.850
of working age has declined for a number of different reasons.

50:49.850 --> 50:52.410
So there's a trend underlying this.

50:52.410 --> 50:57.640
And, in addition, there are probably some people who've left the labor force

50:57.640 --> 51:01.100
just because they are discouraged and they can't find work.

51:01.100 --> 51:04.550
So, as the economy strengthens, the labor market strengthens,

51:04.550 --> 51:09.730
I would expect to see some of these folks coming back into the labor force.

51:09.730 --> 51:14.820
For example, the number of people who are out of the labor force

51:14.820 --> 51:17.430
but say they would like a full-time job but are not actually counted

51:17.430 --> 51:20.630
as unemployed, that number has actually been going up,

51:20.630 --> 51:23.280
which suggests that there are more people thinking about going back

51:23.280 --> 51:26.420
into the labor force, going back to work.

51:26.420 --> 51:29.000
But I doubt that, in the near term at least,

51:29.000 --> 51:33.340
that we'll see an increase in labor force participation because-besides the

51:33.340 --> 51:36.730
effects of the slow recovery, high unemployment,

51:36.730 --> 51:41.170
we've had a downward trend in the U.S., which is not due to the recession,

51:41.170 --> 51:45.330
it's due to underlying demographic factors.

51:45.330 --> 51:48.290
CATHERINE HOLLANDER.

51:48.290 --> 51:53.360
Catherine Hollander from National Journal.

51:53.360 --> 51:57.620
You argued in a 1999 paper and a 2002 speech

51:57.620 --> 52:02.270
that monetary policy was not the right tool for addressing asset bubbles.

52:02.270 --> 52:04.870
But in January, you suggested that there might be a role for it,

52:04.870 --> 52:07.120
even if not as the first line of defense.

52:07.120 --> 52:10.710
Has your thinking on the issue evolved, and can you explain why?

52:10.710 --> 52:12.080
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

52:12.080 --> 52:14.890
Well, I still believe the following,

52:14.890 --> 52:20.140
which is that monetary policy is a very blunt instrument.

52:20.140 --> 52:26.480
If you are raising interest rates to pop an asset bubble,

52:26.480 --> 52:31.120
even if you were sure you can do that, you might, at the same time,

52:31.120 --> 52:32.870
be throwing the economy into recession,

52:32.870 --> 52:39.500
which kind of defeats the purpose of monetary policy.

52:39.500 --> 52:44.750
And therefore, I think the first line of defense-I mean, I think,

52:44.750 --> 52:47.780
we have a sort of a tripartite line of defense.

52:47.780 --> 52:51.140
We start off with very extensive and sophisticated monitoring

52:51.140 --> 52:56.370
at a much higher level and much more comprehensively than we've had in the past.

52:56.370 --> 53:01.210
Then we have supervision and regulation, where we work with other agencies

53:01.210 --> 53:07.070
to try to cover all the empty or uncovered areas in the financial system.

53:07.070 --> 53:17.140
And then, in addition, we try to use communication and similar tools

53:17.140 --> 53:26.270
to affect the way that financial markets respond to monetary policy.

53:26.270 --> 53:32.880
So we do have some first lines of defense, which I think should be used first.

53:32.880 --> 53:39.980
That being said, you know, I think that given the problems

53:39.980 --> 53:44.770
that we've had-not just in the United States, but globally in the last 15,

53:44.770 --> 53:51.020
20 years-that we need to at least take into account these issues

53:51.020 --> 53:52.250
as we make monetary policy.

53:52.250 --> 53:55.090
And I think most of the people on the FOMC would agree with that.

53:55.090 --> 53:57.340
What that means exactly depends on the circumstances.

53:57.340 --> 54:01.660
I think if the economy is in very weak condition and interest rates are very low

54:01.660 --> 54:09.600
for that purpose, it's very difficult to contemplate raising rates a lot

54:09.600 --> 54:13.540
because you're concerned about some sector in the financial sphere.

54:13.540 --> 54:19.680
On the other hand, if you're in an expansion and there's a credit boom going on,

54:19.680 --> 54:21.650
that-the case in that situation

54:21.650 --> 54:27.120
for making policy a little bit tighter might be better.

54:27.120 --> 54:29.910
So, as I've said many times, I have an open mind in this question.

54:29.910 --> 54:32.540
We're learning; all central bankers are learning.

54:32.540 --> 54:37.430
But I think I still would agree with the point I made in my very first speech

54:37.430 --> 54:41.150
in 2002, as a Governor at the Federal Reserve,

54:41.150 --> 54:44.660
where I argued that the first line of defense ought

54:44.660 --> 54:49.990
to be the more targeted tools that we have, including regulatory tools and,

54:49.990 --> 54:54.260
to some extent, macroprudential tools like some emerging markets use.

54:54.260 --> 54:55.820
PETER COOK.

54:55.820 --> 55:00.930
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

55:00.930 --> 55:03.970
As tempted as I might be to end with your NCAA picks

55:03.970 --> 55:07.010
or your view of the Nationals, I have something a little more serious for you,

55:07.010 --> 55:09.830
in line with what John asked you about-your future.

55:09.830 --> 55:13.750
Given the unprecedented nature of Fed policy on your watch

55:13.750 --> 55:16.410
and the uncertainty surrounding the exit strategy,

55:16.410 --> 55:20.080
to what extent do you feel personally responsible to be at the helm

55:20.080 --> 55:23.140
when those decisions are made, and how does that affect your future?

55:23.140 --> 55:24.830
And, more specifically, sir,

55:24.830 --> 55:26.970
the last time we gathered here at the press conference,

55:26.970 --> 55:30.190
you were asked if you'd spoken with the President about your future,

55:30.190 --> 55:31.370
and you said you hadn't at that time.

55:31.370 --> 55:34.100
Could you at least tell us if you've had the conversation?

55:34.100 --> 55:34.690
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

55:34.690 --> 55:36.580
I've spoken to the President a bit,

55:36.580 --> 55:39.900
but I really don't have any-I don't really have any information

55:39.900 --> 55:41.840
for you at this juncture.

55:41.840 --> 55:51.400
I don't think that I'm the only person in the world who can manage the exit.

55:51.400 --> 55:57.020
In fact, one of the things that I hope to accomplish,

55:57.020 --> 56:02.140
and was not entirely successful at, as the Governor or as the Chairman

56:02.140 --> 56:05.900
of the Federal Reserve, was to try to depersonalize, to some extent,

56:05.900 --> 56:11.440
monetary policy and financial policy and to get broader recognition of the fact

56:11.440 --> 56:13.870
that this is an extraordinary institution.

56:13.870 --> 56:17.810
It has a large number of very high quality policymakers.

56:17.810 --> 56:23.080
It has a terrific staff-literally dozens of Ph.D. economists who've been working

56:23.080 --> 56:25.130
through the crisis, trying to understand these issues

56:25.130 --> 56:27.600
and implement our policy tools,

56:27.600 --> 56:33.590
and there's no single person who is essential to that.

56:33.590 --> 56:36.750
But, again, with respect to my personal plans,

56:36.750 --> 56:42.940
I will certainly let you know when I have something more concrete.

56:42.940 --> 56:44.970
Thank you.

